(window.LADDER_CARDS["31a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["31a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"31a1.1","q":"What must a product ⟨f, g⟩ do, what are ‖f‖ and f ⊥ g, and what does the square-sum technique say?","a":"it is straight in the first slot, does not care about order, and has ⟨f, f⟩ > 0 for f ≠ 0; ‖f‖ = √⟨f, f⟩ and f ⊥ g means ⟨f, g⟩ = 0; if f ⊥ g then ‖f + g‖² = ‖f‖² + ‖g‖²","w":["it is straight in both slots and ⟨f, f⟩ ≥ 0; ‖f‖ = ⟨f, f⟩; f ⊥ g means ⟨f, g⟩ = 1; then ‖f + g‖ = ‖f‖ + ‖g‖","it needs only ⟨f, g⟩ = ⟨g, f⟩; ‖f‖ = √⟨f, f⟩; f ⊥ g means f = 0 or g = 0; then ‖f + g‖² = ‖f‖² + ‖g‖²","it must be the dot of two lists; ‖f‖ is the largest entry; f ⊥ g means the lists share no entry; the squares of sizes always add"],"why":"Three demands give a size and a right angle to any space with a product."},
 {"u":"31a2.1","q":"Why does the plain dot fail for lists of turning numbers, what mends it, and what does the turned-product technique say?","a":"(1, i) would have 1·1 + i·i = 0, a size 0 for a vector that is not 0; the second slot is turned back with the conjugate; then the second slot is straight up to conjugates and ⟨f, f⟩ is always real","w":["the dot of turning numbers cannot be computed; the mend is to drop the upright parts; then both slots are straight","(1, i) would have size 2; the mend is to conjugate both slots; then ⟨f, g⟩ = ⟨g, f⟩ again","nothing fails; the conjugate is a habit, and the second slot stays straight with no bar"],"why":"⟨z, z⟩ = |z₁|² + ⋯ + |zₙ|² only with the bar in the second slot."},
 {"u":"31a3.1","q":"What does the product-bound technique say, when is it an equality, and which earlier bounds are cases of it?","a":"|⟨f, g⟩| ≤ ‖f‖ ‖g‖ for any product, with equality exactly when one of f and g is a multiple of the other; 18b1's dot bound in the plane and 8·stat's correlation between −1 and 1 are cases","w":["|⟨f, g⟩| ≥ ‖f‖ ‖g‖, with equality when f ⊥ g; 18b1 and 8·stat are cases","|⟨f, g⟩| ≤ ‖f‖ + ‖g‖, with equality when f = g; the triangle inequality of 8h is the case","|⟨f, g⟩| ≤ ‖f‖ ‖g‖ only for the dot of lists, never an equality; no earlier bound is a case"],"why":"The proof uses only the three demands, so it holds in every product space."},
 {"u":"31a4.1","q":"What does the product-triangle technique say, and how is the angle between two functions defined?","a":"‖f + g‖ ≤ ‖f‖ + ‖g‖, so the size of a difference is a distance; over the reals the angle is the one θ in [0, π] with cos θ = ⟨f, g⟩/(‖f‖ ‖g‖), which exists because the product bound keeps that ratio between −1 and 1","w":["‖f + g‖ = ‖f‖ + ‖g‖; the angle is where the two graphs cross","‖f + g‖ ≤ ‖f‖ + ‖g‖; the angle is ⟨f, g⟩ itself, which can be any real number","‖f + g‖ ≥ ‖f‖ + ‖g‖; the angle is the θ with sin θ = ⟨f, g⟩, and it exists for every product"],"why":"The product bound is what makes the cosine a cosine."},
 {"u":"31a7.1","q":"Why is ∫ x f(x)g(x) dx over [−1, 1] not a product, and why is “⟨f, g⟩ = 0, so f = 0 or g = 0” a move to watch out for?","a":"f = 1 gives ⟨1, 1⟩ = 0, so the weight must be above 0; and 1 and x on [−1, 1] have product 0 with neither being 0: right angles, not zero","w":["it is not straight in the first slot; and ⟨f, g⟩ = 0 does force one of them to be 0 for functions","it cares about order; and ⟨f, g⟩ = 0 only fails for lists of turning numbers","it is a product; and the move fails only when f = g"],"why":"A weight that changes sign breaks the third demand."},
 {"u":"31a5.1","q":"What is [f, g], and how does it give ⟨f, g⟩ back? Why does a map that keeps every size keep every product?","a":"the floor part of ⟨f, g⟩, a product over the reals; ⟨f, g⟩ = [f, g] + i[f, ig]; the product is built from four sizes, ‖f + g‖, ‖f − g‖, ‖f + ig‖ and ‖f − ig‖, so keeping sizes keeps it","w":["the size of ⟨f, g⟩; ⟨f, g⟩ = [f, g]²; because sizes are products","the upright part of ⟨f, g⟩; ⟨f, g⟩ = i[f, g]; because every map keeps 0","the product of the floor parts of f and g; it cannot be given back; it does not"],"why":"31a5: ⟨f, ig⟩ = −i⟨f, g⟩, whose floor part is the upright part of ⟨f, g⟩."},
 {"u":"31a6.1","q":"What is the grid of a product in a basis, and how does it give ⟨f, g⟩? What is the grid when the basis is right-angled?","a":"the grid G with Gᵢⱼ = ⟨vⱼ, vᵢ⟩; then ⟨f, g⟩ = y*Gx for the readings x of f and y of g; for a right-angled basis G = I and the product is the dot of the readings","w":["the grid of sizes ‖vᵢ‖ down the middle; ⟨f, g⟩ = xᵀy always; G = 0","the slope grid of the basis; ⟨f, g⟩ = det G; G is a staircase","the grid P of the basis vectors; ⟨f, g⟩ = Px · Py; G = P"],"why":"31a6: multiply out ⟨Σ xⱼvⱼ, Σ yᵢvᵢ⟩, straight in the first slot and turned in the second."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["31b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["31b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"31b1.1","q":"What does the closest-point technique say, and what is the shadow of f on the line through g for any g ≠ 0?","a":"if ‖e‖ = 1, the closest point of the line through e to f is ⟨f, e⟩e, and the miss is at right angles to e; for any g ≠ 0 the shadow is (⟨f, g⟩/‖g‖²)g","w":["the closest point is ⟨f, e⟩e for any e; for any g the shadow is ⟨f, g⟩g","the closest point is e itself; the shadow on g is g/‖g‖","the closest point is ⟨f, e⟩e with the miss along e; the shadow on g is (⟨f, g⟩/‖g‖)g"],"why":"Cut g to size 1 first: e = g/‖g‖."},
 {"u":"31b2.1","q":"What is a right-angled list, what does the shadow-sum technique say, and why is the shadow of eˣ on the lines over [−1, 1] not its tangent line at 0?","a":"vectors of size 1, each at right angles to the others; the closest point of what they build is Pf = Σ⟨f, eₖ⟩eₖ, with the miss at right angles to the whole space; the tangent line is best at one point, the shadow is best over the interval","w":["any free list; the closest point is Σ⟨f, vₖ⟩vₖ; the shadow and the tangent line are the same line","vectors at right angles of any size; Pf = Σ⟨f, eₖ⟩eₖ; the tangent line is closer, since it touches","vectors of size 1; the closest point is the largest single ⟨f, eₖ⟩eₖ; the shadow is the tangent at the middle"],"why":"Closest under the area product means least total squared miss."},
 {"u":"31b3.1","q":"What does the miss-at-right-angles technique say, what is G, and why is G undoable?","a":"for free v₁, …, vₖ, w = Σcⱼvⱼ is closest to f exactly when Gc = b, with Gᵢⱼ = ⟨vⱼ, vᵢ⟩ and bᵢ = ⟨f, vᵢ⟩; Gc = 0 gives ‖Σcⱼvⱼ‖² = 0, so every cⱼ is 0 because the vⱼ are free","w":["w is closest when c = b, with no grid; G is the identity","w is closest exactly when Gc = b with Gᵢⱼ = ⟨vⱼ, vᵢ⟩; G is undoable because every product is","w is closest when the miss is along W; G is the grid of sizes ‖vⱼ‖, undoable when none is 0"],"why":"Right angles to each vᵢ, asked one at a time, are k samenesses in k unknowns."},
 {"u":"31b4.1","q":"In what space, and under what product, is the best line through n points a shadow, and which residual facts of 13·stat are right angles?","a":"in lists of n numbers under the dot: the fitted values k1 + bx are the shadow of y on the flat piece built by 1 and x; Σeᵢ = 0 is e ⊥ 1 and Σxᵢeᵢ = 0 is e ⊥ x","w":["in the plane of the graph under the dot: the line is the shadow of the points on the x-axis; the residuals are at right angles to the line drawn","in functions on an interval under the area product; Σeᵢ² = 0 is the right angle","in lists of n numbers: the line is the shadow of x on y; Σeᵢ = 0 says e ⊥ y"],"why":"Least squares is closest point, with one place for each data point."},
 {"u":"31b5.1","q":"Why does the shadow-sum formula fail for a basis that is not right-angled, and why does the best line not show that x causes y?","a":"with other angles the readings ⟨f, vₖ⟩ count shared directions twice, so Gc = b must be solved; and a shadow is geometry of the two lists, which says nothing about what drives what","w":["it does not fail, it is only slower; and the best line shows cause when the fit is close","it fails only when a vₖ has size 0; and the line shows cause when its slope is positive","it fails because bases have no shadows; and cause needs a second line through the same data"],"why":"The formula used ⟨eⱼ, eₖ⟩ = 0 for j ≠ k."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["31c"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["31c"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"31c1.1","q":"How is eₖ built from vₖ and e₁, …, eₖ₋₁, what does the straightening technique say, and why is wₖ never 0?","a":"wₖ is vₖ less its shadows on e₁, …, eₖ₋₁, and eₖ = wₖ/‖wₖ‖; the eₖ are a right-angled list, and the first k build the same space as v₁, …, vₖ; wₖ = 0 would put vₖ in the space of the earlier ones, against free","w":["eₖ = vₖ/‖vₖ‖; the eₖ are right-angled for any list; wₖ is never 0 because sizes are positive","wₖ is vₖ less its shadow on eₖ₋₁ alone; the list is right-angled; wₖ ≠ 0 by the product bound","eₖ is vₖ less every earlier vⱼ; the eₖ build a smaller space; wₖ can be 0 and is then skipped"],"why":"Take away what is already built, then cut to size 1."},
 {"u":"31c2.1","q":"What are the first three straightened powers of x on [−1, 1], up to size, and why does the closest cubic leave the first three terms of the closest parabola unchanged?","a":"1, x and x² − 1/3; along a right-angled list each reading is found by its own product, so adding a fourth direction leaves the first three as they were","w":["1, x and x²; a cubic fit refits every number","1, x − 1 and x² − x; the readings are kept because polynomials are free","1, x and x² − 1/3; the readings change, but only a little"],"why":"⟨1, x²⟩ = 2/3 is the one shadow to take away."},
 {"u":"31c3.1","q":"What does the column-straightening technique say, what are the numbers of R, and how does X = QR help with XᵀXc = Xᵀy?","a":"X = QR with right-angled columns in Q and R a staircase with numbers above 0 down its middle; Rⱼₖ = ⟨vₖ, eⱼ⟩ above the middle and Rₖₖ = ‖wₖ‖; the samenesses become Rc = Qᵀy, solved from the bottom up","w":["X = QR with Q a staircase and R right-angled; R holds the sizes of the columns; the samenesses become Qc = y","X = QR for square X only; R is the identity; the samenesses are unchanged","X = QR with QQᵀ = I always; R holds the stretches of X; the samenesses become c = Ry"],"why":"QᵀQ = I removes the Q from both sides."},
 {"u":"31c4.1","q":"Why is w₂ = v₂ − ⟨v₂, v₁⟩v₁ wrong when ‖v₁‖ ≠ 1, and why is QQᵀ not I when Q has fewer columns than rows?","a":"the shadow on v₁ is ⟨v₂, v₁⟩v₁/‖v₁‖², so v₁ must be cut to size 1 first; and with fewer columns QQᵀ is the shadow map onto what the columns build, not the do-nothing map","w":["it is right for every v₁; and QQᵀ = I whenever QᵀQ = I","the sign is wrong; and QQᵀ is the zero grid","the shadow needs v₂ of size 1; and QQᵀ is not defined for such Q"],"why":"A shadow map kills the directions the columns do not reach."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["31d"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["31d"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"31d1.1","q":"When is a straight map symmetric, what does that mean for a real grid under the dot, and what does the real-stretches technique say?","a":"when ⟨Af, g⟩ = ⟨f, Ag⟩ for all f and g; for a real grid, Aᵀ = A; and every stretch of a symmetric map is a real number","w":["when ⟨Af, Ag⟩ = ⟨f, g⟩; for a real grid, AᵀA = I; every stretch has size 1","when A = A⁻¹; for a real grid, A² = I; every stretch is ±1","when ⟨Af, g⟩ = ⟨f, Ag⟩; for a real grid, every number is positive; every stretch is positive"],"why":"λ⟨v, v⟩ = ⟨Av, v⟩ = ⟨v, Av⟩ = λ̄⟨v, v⟩."},
 {"u":"31d2.1","q":"What does the right-angled-stretches technique say, and which line of its proof needs the stretches to be real?","a":"if a symmetric map stretches u by λ and v by μ with λ ≠ μ, then u ⊥ v; moving A across gives μ̄⟨u, v⟩, and that is μ⟨u, v⟩ only because μ is real","w":["vectors with the same stretch are at right angles; the line that divides by λ","different stretches give free vectors and no more; no line needs real stretches","different stretches give u ⊥ v for every grid; the line Au = λu needs λ real"],"why":"(λ − μ)⟨u, v⟩ = 0 with λ ≠ μ."},
 {"u":"31d3.1","q":"What does the spectral technique say, what are Q and D, and why does A keep the space at right angles to e₁?","a":"a symmetric map has a right-angled basis it only stretches, by real numbers; A = QDQᵀ with Q a length-keeper whose columns are the axes and D the stretches down the middle; if v ⊥ e₁ then ⟨Av, e₁⟩ = ⟨v, Ae₁⟩ = λ₁⟨v, e₁⟩ = 0","w":["every grid has such a basis; Q is any undoable grid and D its determinant; A keeps every space","a symmetric map has n different stretches; Q holds the stretches and D the axes; A sends that space to e₁","a symmetric map has a basis it only stretches, not at right angles; A = QDQ⁻¹ only; the space is kept because A is undoable"],"why":"Induction on the dimension, inside the space at right angles to the first axis."},
 {"u":"31d4.1","q":"What does the product-keeper technique say, what is U*, and why does every stretch of a product-keeper have size 1?","a":"U keeps every product exactly when U*U = I, where U* is the transpose with every number conjugated; then ‖Uv‖ = ‖v‖, so Uv = λv gives |λ| ‖v‖ = ‖v‖","w":["U keeps products exactly when U* = U; U* is the undoing; stretches are real","U keeps products when det U = 1; U* is the transpose; stretches are 1","U keeps products exactly when U*U = I; U* is the conjugate with no transpose; stretches have size 1 because they are real"],"why":"Keeping sizes pins every stretch to the circle of size 1."},
 {"u":"31d9.1","q":"Why does A = PDP⁻¹ not give columns of P at right angles, and why does the quarter turn have no real stretch though it keeps lengths?","a":"right-angled axes are promised only for symmetric A: rows (1, 1), (0, 2) have stretched vectors at 45°; the quarter turn is a keeper, not symmetric, and its stretches are i and −i, of size 1","w":["the columns of P are always at right angles; the quarter turn has stretch 1","P has right-angled columns when D has different numbers; the quarter turn has no stretches at all","right angles need det A = 1; the quarter turn has real stretches ±1"],"why":"Symmetric gives real stretches; keeper gives stretches of size 1."},
 {"u":"31d5.1","q":"What is the moved-across map of A? What does A* reach?","a":"the one map A* with ⟨Af, g⟩ = ⟨f, A*g⟩ for all f and g; in a right-angled basis its grid is A’s on its side with conjugates; it reaches exactly the vectors at right angles to everything A sends to 0","w":["the map that undoes A; it reaches everything A reaches","A with every entry turned to its conjugate, and no flip; it reaches nothing","the symmetric part of A; it reaches the stretches of A"],"why":"31d5: A*g = Σ⟨g, Aeₖ⟩eₖ moves A across, and Af = 0 exactly when f is at right angles to every A*g."},
 {"u":"31d6.1","q":"What do symmetric maps and product-keepers have in common? Give three ways of saying that A swaps with its moved-across map.","a":"each swaps with its own moved-across map; AA* = A*A; ‖Af‖ = ‖A*f‖ for every f; A has a right-angled basis that it only stretches","w":["each has only real stretches; A = A*; A² = I; A keeps every size","each keeps every length; A*A = I; det A = 1; A is a turn","each is its own undoing; A = A⁻¹; A has no stretch; A is diagonal in every basis"],"why":"31d6: ‖Af‖² = ⟨A*Af, f⟩ and ‖A*f‖² = ⟨AA*f, f⟩; and a stretched vector of A is a stretched vector of A*, so the space at right angles to it is kept."},
 {"u":"31d7.1","q":"What does U = (A − i)(A + i)⁻¹ do to the stretches of a symmetric A? Which point of the circle is never a stretch of U?","a":"it sends each real stretch λ to (λ − i)/(λ + i), a number of size 1, so U is a product-keeper; 1 is never a stretch of U, since 1 − U = 2i(A + i)⁻¹ can be undone","w":["it squares them; −1 is never a stretch of U","it turns each to its conjugate; i is never a stretch of U","it sends them all to 1; 0 is never a stretch of U"],"why":"31d7: ‖(A − i)f‖² and ‖(A + i)f‖² are both ‖Af‖² + ‖f‖², because the cross terms cancel for a symmetric A."},
 {"u":"31d8.1","q":"What does a map that keeps every distance and fixes 0 turn out to be? How does the proof get T(f + g) = Tf + Tg?","a":"a straight map that keeps every product; products are built from distances, so they are kept, and then ‖T(f + g) − Tf − Tg‖², multiplied out into products, equals ‖(f + g) − f − g‖² = 0","w":["a slide; from T0 = 0 alone","the identity; by taking slopes of T","a symmetric map; from the spectral technique"],"why":"31d8: 2⟨Tf, Tg⟩ = ‖Tf‖² + ‖Tg‖² − ‖Tf − Tg‖², and each term on the right is a kept distance."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["31e"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["31e"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"31e1.1","q":"What is a quadratic form, why may A be taken symmetric, and what does the axes-form technique say?","a":"q(x) = xᵀAx; any grid G gives the same q as ½(G + Gᵀ); with A = QDQᵀ and y = Qᵀx, q = λ₁y₁² + ⋯ + λₙyₙ², a sum of squares along A's axes","w":["q(x) = Ax; A is symmetric by definition of a grid; q is the product of the stretches","q(x) = xᵀAx; A must be symmetric or q is not defined; q = (λ₁y₁ + ⋯ + λₙyₙ)²","q(x) = xᵀAx; G and Gᵀ give different forms; along the axes q has only cross terms"],"why":"The cross terms vanish along the axes."},
 {"u":"31e2.1","q":"What does the sign-count technique say, and why must U and U′ in its proof share a vector other than 0?","a":"however q is written as a sum of squares after an undoable change, the counts of numbers above 0, below 0 and equal to 0 are the same; U (where one way is positive) and U′ (where the other is not) have dimensions adding to more than n","w":["the numbers in front are always the same; U and U′ are the same space","the count above 0 can change but the total cannot; U and U′ share a vector because both hold 0","the signs are the same only along the axes; U and U′ are at right angles"],"why":"On a shared vector q would be above 0 and not above 0 at once."},
 {"u":"31e3.1","q":"What is a positive definite form, what does the form-test technique say, and why is det A > 0 alone not enough for n = 2?","a":"q(x) > 0 for every x ≠ 0; that holds exactly when every stretch of A is above 0, for n = 2 when p > 0 and pr − s² > 0; det > 0 only says the two stretches share a sign, and both may be below 0","w":["q(x) ≥ 0 for every x; when det A > 0; the determinant is enough","q(x) > 0 for some x; when some stretch is above 0; det > 0 fails only for s = 0","q(x) > 0 for x ≠ 0; when every number of A is above 0; det > 0 says nothing about signs"],"why":"−x₁² − x₂² has determinant 1."},
 {"u":"31e4.1","q":"What does the hug-axes technique say, and what decides when some stretch of H is 0?","a":"at a flat point, all stretches of H above 0 give a bottom, all below 0 a top, both signs a saddle; along an axis with stretch 0 the second-order part is flat and the leftover E decides","w":["all stretches above 0 give a top; a stretch 0 gives a saddle","the sign of det H alone decides; a stretch 0 means the point is not flat","both signs give a bottom; with a stretch 0 the first slopes decide"],"why":"x² + y⁴ has a bottom and x² − y⁴ a saddle, with the same H."},
 {"u":"31e10.1","q":"Why does det A > 0 not make q positive definite, and why can the numbers in front change when their signs cannot?","a":"−x₁² − x₂² has det 1 and is below 0 away from 0; an undoable change of directions rescales the squares, as 3 and 1 along the axes against 2 and 3/2 by completing the square, and the sign-count technique fixes only the counts of signs","w":["det A > 0 does make it positive definite; the numbers in front never change","det > 0 fails only for grids with 0s; the numbers change because A is not symmetric","−x₁² − x₂² has det −1; the signs can change too, with a flip"],"why":"Counts of signs are what every way of writing q shares."},
 {"u":"31e5.1","q":"What does the corner-determinants technique test, and what does it show? Why can a grid with every Dₖ > 0 not have two stretches below 0?","a":"the determinants of the top-left corners of every size: all above 0 exactly when the form is positive definite; two such stretches would give a vector with last place 0 on which q is below 0, though q is positive on the corner of n − 1 rows","w":["the entries down the middle: all above 0 exactly when A can be undone; because stretches come in conjugate pairs","the determinant of A alone: above 0 exactly when q is positive definite; because det A is the sum of the stretches","the corners from the bottom right only; because the last place is always 0"],"why":"31e5: by induction the corner of n − 1 rows is positive definite, Dₙ > 0 makes the number of stretches below 0 even, and two of them give a contradiction."},
 {"u":"31e6.1","q":"How is a positive definite grid a square? What does P do to the product whose grid is A?","a":"A = PᵀP for a P that can be undone, and P can be the one staircase R with numbers above 0 down its middle; then xᵀAy = (Px)ᵀ(Py): the product is the plain dot of Px and Py","w":["A = P² for a symmetric P only; P undoes the product","A = P + Pᵀ; P halves the product","A = PP⁻¹; P keeps every length"],"why":"31e6: xᵀPᵀPx = |Px|²; and from A = QDQᵀ, P = √D Qᵀ works, and straightening its columns gives the staircase."},
 {"u":"31e7.1","q":"How many never-negative symmetric roots has a never-negative symmetric map? Why must any such root agree with Q√D Qᵀ?","a":"exactly one; a root T stretches each of its axes by some t ≥ 0, A stretches that axis by t², so on each stretched space of A the root is √λ times the identity, as Q√D Qᵀ is","w":["two, S and −S; because (−S)² = S²","one for each basis; because Q can be chosen freely","none unless A is diagonal; because roots do not swap"],"why":"31e7: Tv = tv gives Av = t²v, so the stretched vectors of T sort themselves into the stretched spaces of A."},
 {"u":"31e8.1","q":"What are P and U in A = UP? What does each do to space?","a":"P = √(AᵀA), positive definite and symmetric, and U = AP⁻¹, a length-keeper; P stretches along right-angled axes by factors above 0, and U then turns or mirrors with no change of shape","w":["P is the staircase of A and U its row moves; P slides and U stretches","P = AᵀA and U = A⁻¹; P squares lengths and U undoes A","P is the diagonal of A and U the rest; P keeps the axes and U mixes them"],"why":"31e8: UᵀU = P⁻¹AᵀAP⁻¹ = I, and any split A = U′P′ forces P′² = AᵀA, which has one positive root."},
 {"u":"31e9.1","q":"When is A ≤ B for symmetric maps? Name one thing the order of numbers has that this order lacks.","a":"when B − A is never-negative: ⟨Af, f⟩ ≤ ⟨Bf, f⟩ for every f; not every pair compares, and A ≤ B does not give A² ≤ B²","w":["when every entry of A is at most the entry of B; it has no least member","when det A ≤ det B; it does not chain","when A is a corner of B; it is not kept by adding"],"why":"31e9: the chain and the sameness come from ⟨(B − A)f, f⟩ ≥ 0, and the turn-over for undoings from I ≤ M giving M⁻¹ ≤ I on the axes of M."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["31f"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["31f"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"31f1.1","q":"What does the never-negative technique say, and what are the stretch sizes of A, with m rows and n columns?","a":"the stretches of AᵀA are never below 0, since λ = |Av|² for an axis v of size 1, and Av = 0 exactly when AᵀAv = 0; the stretch sizes of A are the roots σₖ = √λₖ","w":["the stretches of A are never below 0; the stretch sizes are the stretches of A","the stretches of AᵀA are the squares of the stretches of A; the stretch sizes are their sizes","AᵀA has stretches of both signs; the stretch sizes are the λₖ themselves"],"why":"vᵀAᵀAv is a squared length."},
 {"u":"31f2.1","q":"What does the turn-stretch-turn technique say, and where do the vₖ, uₖ and σₖ come from?","a":"A = UΣVᵀ with U, V length-keepers and Avₖ = σₖuₖ; the vₖ are right-angled axes of AᵀA, σₖ = √λₖ, and uₖ = Avₖ/σₖ, at right angles because (Avⱼ)·(Avₖ) = λₖ vⱼ·vₖ = 0","w":["A = UΣU⁻¹; the vₖ are the stretched vectors of A; the uₖ are the same vectors","A = UΣVᵀ only for square A; the vₖ are any basis; the uₖ are straightened afterwards","A = UΣVᵀ with Σ holding the stretches of A; the uₖ are axes of A + Aᵀ"],"why":"Every grid, of any shape: turn, stretch along axes, turn."},
 {"u":"31f3.1","q":"What does the greatest-stretch technique say, what is ‖A‖, and why does the proof need the uₖ at right angles?","a":"|Ax| ≤ σ₁|x| with equality at v₁, and ‖A‖ = σ₁; the square-sum technique gives |Σyₖσₖuₖ|² = Σσₖ²yₖ² only for right angles","w":["|Ax| ≤ λ₁|x| with λ₁ the largest stretch of A; ‖A‖ is the largest number in A; right angles are not needed","|Ax| = σ₁|x| for every x; ‖A‖ = det A; the uₖ must be free","|Ax| ≥ σ₁|x|; ‖A‖ is the smallest stretch size; right angles make Σ diagonal"],"why":"With cross terms the squared sizes would not add."},
 {"u":"31f4.1","q":"What is Aᵣ, what does the keep-the-largest technique say, and why must some x ≠ 0 be lost by B and built by v₁, …, vᵣ₊₁?","a":"Aᵣ is the first r pieces σₖuₖvₖᵀ; ‖A − Aᵣ‖ = σᵣ₊₁ and no grid reaching at most r dimensions is closer; B loses at least n − r dimensions, and that with r + 1 more is over n","w":["Aᵣ is A with all but r rows set to 0; it is closest among grids with r rows; x exists because B is undoable","Aᵣ is the first r pieces; ‖A − Aᵣ‖ = σᵣ; x exists because the vₖ are free","Aᵣ is the r largest numbers of A; no grid is closer; x is any vector B stretches"],"why":"Two spaces whose dimensions add to more than n share a vector."},
 {"u":"31f5.1","q":"Why are the stretch sizes of A not its stretches, and why is ‖A‖ not the largest number in A?","a":"the grid of 31f2 has stretches 3 and 5 and stretch sizes 6.708 and 2.236; rows (1, 1), (1, 1) have largest number 1 and ‖A‖ = 2","w":["they are the same for every grid; ‖A‖ is the largest number","stretch sizes are the squares of the stretches; ‖A‖ is the sum of the numbers","they differ only for symmetric grids; ‖A‖ is the largest number when all are positive"],"why":"Stretch sizes come from AᵀA; they match the stretches only for symmetric A with none below 0."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["31g"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["31g"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"31g1.1","q":"What are the three demands on a size, and which line shows that no product gives the highest height ‖f‖∞?","a":"‖f‖ ≥ 0 with 0 only for f = 0, ‖tf‖ = |t| ‖f‖, and ‖f + g‖ ≤ ‖f‖ + ‖g‖; f = x and g = 1 − x on [0, 1] break the parallelogram line, 1² + 1² ≠ 2·1² + 2·1²","w":["only ‖f + g‖ ≤ ‖f‖ + ‖g‖; the line ‖x‖∞ = 1","the three demands of a product; no line shows it, since ‖f‖∞ = √⟨f, f⟩","‖f‖ > 0 always, ‖tf‖ = t‖f‖, ‖fg‖ = ‖f‖ ‖g‖; the line ‖x‖₁ = 1/2"],"why":"Sizes from a product obey ‖f + g‖² + ‖f − g‖² = 2‖f‖² + 2‖g‖²."},
 {"u":"31g2.1","q":"What does the size-order technique say, what are matched powers, and what does the matched-powers technique say?","a":"on an interval of length L, ‖f‖₁ ≤ √L ‖f‖₂ ≤ L ‖f‖∞; p and q are matched when 1/p + 1/q = 1; then ∫|fg| ≤ ‖f‖ₚ ‖g‖_q","w":["‖f‖∞ ≤ ‖f‖₂ ≤ ‖f‖₁; matched means p = q; then ∫|fg| = ‖f‖ₚ ‖g‖ₚ","‖f‖₁ ≤ ‖f‖₂ ≤ ‖f‖∞ on every interval; matched means p + q = 1; then ∫|fg| ≥ ‖f‖ₚ ‖g‖_q","the three sizes are equal on [0, 1]; matched means pq = 1; then ∫|fg| ≤ ‖f‖ₚ + ‖g‖_q"],"why":"The inequalities run one way only: the thin tents show it."},
 {"u":"31g3.1","q":"Why does the first demand of a size fail for ‖·‖₁ on all sized functions, and how does the ladder mend it?","a":"h = 1 at ½ and 0 elsewhere has ‖h‖₁ = 0 and is not the zero function, so limits would stop being one thing; functions that agree but for a set of size zero are taken as the same, and on those boxes ‖f‖₁ = 0 only for f = 0","w":["‖h‖₁ is not defined for h; the ladder allows only continuous functions","the triangle inequality fails for h; the ladder drops the same-members axiom","h has ‖h‖₁ = 1; the ladder sets h(½) = 0 by technique"],"why":"A space built on totals cannot see one point."},
 {"u":"31g4.1","q":"When does a list of functions crowd and settle under a size, what does the size-complete technique say, and why do continuous functions under ‖·‖₁ not have it?","a":"it crowds when ‖fₙ − fₘ‖ → 0 and settles at f when ‖fₙ − f‖ → 0; in L¹ and L² every crowding list settles, and a sub-list settles at every point but a set of size zero; the ramps over [½, ½ + 1/n] crowd and settle at a step, which is not continuous","w":["it crowds when fₙ(x) settles at each x; L¹ has holes; continuous functions are complete under ‖·‖₁","it crowds when ‖fₙ‖ → 0; in L¹ every list settles; the ramps do not crowd","it crowds when ‖fₙ − fₘ‖ → 0; in L¹ the whole list settles at every point; continuous functions fail because they are unbounded"],"why":"Rung 30's totals fill the holes."},
 {"u":"31g5.1","q":"Why is f(½) not a number for f in L², and why does settling in total size not give settling at each point?","a":"the box of f holds functions with every value at ½; and the narrow blocks that sweep across [0, 1] again and again settle at 0 in total size and at no single point","w":["f(½) is the average of f; settling in total size does give settling at each point","f(½) is always 0 in L²; the sweeping blocks settle at ½","f(½) is a number once f is continuous somewhere; settling fails only on a set of size zero"],"why":"Only a sub-list is promised to settle point by point."});
